      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_f127f78b2b537c14fe6c0b38e1d63bdc508aff34f30bfac192d6d01a0939341b",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "The zeta-field construction and the difference-Hamiltonian audit distinguish factor labels from operator spectrum. In curved space the source acknowledges that completed zeta of the d'Alembertian lacks the flat Fourier definition and proposes a regularized zero factorization followed by a formal sum of local second-order fields. The other program shows, for a different operator, that formal eigenfunctions do not establish a common domain, true adjoint, completeness, or self-adjointness. Factorization can expose zero-indexed factors, but it does not by itself supply reconstruction, convergence, covariance, or a self-adjoint spectral realization.",
